  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 404 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nLow stratus continues across the region slowly eroding across \neastern Montana and trying to scatter out across central North \nDakota. Surface high pressure continues to filter in across the west \naiding in the clearing skies. There is still a chance all the low \nstratus does not clear out tonight which will cause low temperatures \nto be a few degrees warmer then currently forecast. Low temperatures \ntonight are forecast to drop down into below freezing, as low as 25 \ndegrees in some spots, which could result in a widespread freeze. \nHowever, if the cloud cover continues to linger across the area, \nclouds could foil any widespread freeze. Tomorrow, high temperatures \nare forecast to sit in the 50s to lower 60s across the state. Low \ntemperatures could be near freezing as surface high pressure moves \noff to the east. \n\nAn upper level low over the Desert Southwest will move up into the \nCentral Plains Thursday with another upper level low dropping out \nfrom the Canadian Prairies. This split flow pattern will place North \nDakota on the equatorward side of the northern stream, favoring \nstrong cold air advection on the backside of the upper low. Thus \nwindy conditions are forecast across the state Thursday with winds \naround 25 to 35 mph in the afternoon with some gusts up to 50 mph. \nWinds will tapper off by the overnight hours. There could also be a \nshort window with relative humidity around 30 percent and windy \nconditions leading to near critical to critical fire weather \nconditions Thursday afternoon. There is a low chance of maybe some \nshowers in the James River Valley early Thursday morning. Surface \nhigh pressure will build over western North Dakota Thursday night \ninto Friday morning resulting in another widespread freeze across \nthe state.\n\nAfter this upper low moves out of the region and strengthens over \nthe Great Lakes, an upper level ridge will build over western CONUS. \nThis ridge will kick surface high pressure off to the east resulting \nin the north south pressure gradient Friday. Which will cause breezy \nconditions out west. Winds are forecast to be around 15-20 mph \noverlapping with 20 percent minimum relative humidity could result \nin another afternoon of near critical fire weather conditions. The \nupper level ridge will center over the Northern Plains leading to \nwarmer temperatures in the 60s to 70s this weekend into next week. \nNo major system are forecast to move through the region this weekend \nresulting in dry and warm conditions.",
